+# Flight Logbook
+
+> Institutional memory. Concise, factual, high-signal.
+> Newest entries first. One block per insight.
+
+## 2026-09-02
+
+### 2110 — A specification's controlled vocabulary is a mapping, not a reading
+
+- FINDING: `core_records.go` validated Session Event `predecessors` with `validateSortedUniqueDigests`, so a duplicate predecessor was **refused**. Pinned Section 5.2 (SPEC.md:1728) declares "`predecessors` as a **sorted** array of one or more record/event digests". Section 1.6 (SPEC.md:323) defines `sorted unique T[n..m]` as the COMPOUND phrase meaning "bytewise canonical ordering **and no duplicate**", and the document applies that phrase systematically — `event_heads`, `manifest_ids`, `evidence_ids`, `object_ids`, `accepted_risks`, `sanitized_remote_urls`, `agent_project_config_paths` — while using bare `sorted` for `predecessors` alone. Reading the two as synonyms invented a refusal. This was the FIFTH invented constraint on this leaf; the previous four were each fixed one at a time.
+- FINDING, found by the new gate rather than by review: the SAME error sat in `validateWorkspaceGroupRecord`. Section 2 (SPEC.md:2146) declares "Members are sorted by `workspace_id`, and no two members may have an equal or case-colliding `group_relative_path`" — the declared uniqueness is on `group_relative_path`, never on `workspace_id`. Production refused two members sharing a `workspace_id`. Repaired, and the admission is now pinned by an accepting case at both identity entries.
+- ROOT CAUSE, one level up from "read the spec more carefully": the document uses a CONTROLLED VOCABULARY for array constraints and the implementation treated one of its phrases as a synonym of another. Judgement is the failure mode, so judgement had to leave the loop.
+- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/array_order_inventory_test.go` plus `testdata/array-order-constraints.md`. Ordering sites are derived structurally — a comparison between a value derived from the ranged element and a value bound to an EARLIER element, either carried forward or indexed at an offset from the loop key — so no rename hides one. Strength is read off the comparison OPERATOR (`<=` refuses duplicates, `<` admits them), and the member name is derived by tracing the ranged collection back to the string literal that named it, through helper parameters and across call sites. Order and uniqueness are pinned as SEPARATE factors with their own SPEC lines, because the document frequently declares them at different lines; `sorted unique` must cite a uniqueness declaration and bare `sorted` must cite none. A strengthened validator then cannot be written down consistently.
+- NOTE: the gate proves its own coverage three ways rather than one. A site whose member cannot be traced is REPORTED, which is what a new ordering helper with no call site looks like — an unpinned rule that contributes no row and would otherwise slip past a sequence comparison entirely. Every production refusal whose message speaks about order must belong to a function carrying a derived site, which catches a check written in a shape the tracer does not model. And the mapping is a pure function with its own negative proof.
+- NOTE, a survivor worth recording: the first version of that negative proof asserted only "some problem was reported". Narrowing the missing-uniqueness-citation clause SURVIVED, because the neighbouring default branch reported a different problem for the same row. Each case now pins the exact message it must produce, and a further mutant confirmed the matcher itself is exact. An isolated case must pin its own clause, not the fact that something complained.
+- DISCLOSURE, not repaired here: `WorkspaceSnapshot.members` and `GitIndex.entries` enforce uniqueness the pinned document declares nowhere — an equal `workspace_id` and an equal `(path, stage)` pair respectively. Both sit in `closed_shapes.go`, in sections owned by `TASK-260830-uqnwmi`, inside a reviewed and accepted candidate. They are recorded as disclosed strengthenings with their exact undeclared refusal and routed, and the disclosed set is asserted exactly against the artifact in both directions so a fourth cannot appear silently.
+- EVIDENCE: 26 mutants, 26 killed, 0 survivors, guarded baseline green before and after. Twelve flip an ordering operator at every derived site in the package (six strengthenings, six widenings), five attack the provenance checker itself, five edit the pinned artifact, and two add a second ordering helper and an order refusal in an unmodelled shape as new production files.
+- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/`, `internal/traceability/`, `README.md`, `LOGBOOK.md`
+- STATUS: Resolved; TASK-260830-1tax26 handed to review as revision 7.
+
+
+### 1730 — A mutant grammar decides what a sweep can find; two shapes had none
+
+- FINDING: two live gates in `internal/canonicaljson/core_records.go` were proven in one direction only, and review reproduced both end to end against the whole configured gate set. Narrowing `requiresError != errorPresent` to `requiresError && !errorPresent` made `ValidateObservationEvent` **return nil and attest** a `success` Observation Event carrying `"error_code":"boom"`. Narrowing `epoch != 1` to `epoch >= 3` made `CalculateObjectIdentity` **attest** an epoch-2 Lease Record with a null `predecessor_lease_id`. `go test ./...`, `tracecheck` and all four seeded fuzz targets stayed green through both.
+- ROOT CAUSE, one level up from "we missed a case": the previous three sweeps rewrote comparison OPERATORS and deleted guards. Neither operation can express either shape. A boolean-valued `!=` has no operator worth rewriting — the mutant is a split of the identity into its two single-sided halves. And `epoch != 1` narrowed to `epoch > 1` is an EQUIVALENT mutant in a positive-integer domain, so an operator sweep reports a kill and says nothing; only a mutant carrying a different LITERAL (`>= K+2`, `!= K+1`) can move the boundary. A sweep proves exactly as much as its grammar can say.
+- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/coupling_derivation_test.go` derives both subjects from the production sources. Boolean-valued is decided from the source — a negation, a nested comparison, a short-circuit, or an identifier the enclosing function binds from a package function whose declared result at that position is `bool` — so the coupling set is not a list. Integer-literal comparisons need no type inference at all: Go refuses to compile a comparison between an untyped integer constant and a non-numeric operand, so the compiler has already established the other side. `coupling_proofs_test.go` obliges two single-sided proofs per coupling (the pair chosen by the operator) and one proof at each value where a literal comparison flips (read off the operator), and asserts obligations equal proofs in both directions.
+- NOTE: the derived key carries the literal, so moving a literal fails the gate BEFORE any case runs. Moving `epoch != 1` to `epoch != 5` was verified to redden the coverage assertion rather than merely one case.
+- DECISION: the Observation result/error and tombstone couplings are driven for EVERY value the production vocabulary admits, resolved through the same derivation the closed-vocabulary inventory uses, rather than for one hand-picked value. Admit by derivation, refuse the complement.
+- DECISION: three boundary values are unreachable and declared, not dropped — a zero `sequence` and a zero `epoch` are refused by the positive-integer gate first. Each names its subsuming refusal and a test that must exist. The one function whose comparisons run at package initialization over the pinned catalog is exempted by name, and the exemption asserts from the sources that every caller of it is a package-level initializer that panics.
+- EVIDENCE: 40 mutants generated from that same derivation — both single-direction narrowings of every derived coupling, and literal shifts an operator grammar cannot express — 40 killed, 0 survivors, green baseline before and after. Five self-checks confirm the gates can fail: a deleted coupling proof, a deleted boundary proof, a duplicated claim, a moved production literal, and a narrowed derivation each redden the suite.
+- SCOPE, stated rather than implied: the literal-boundary gate covers `core_records.go`, this leaf's file. `canonical.go` and `closed_shapes.go` remain owned by the sibling `TASK-260830-uqnwmi`. The presence-coupling gate is package-wide, because eight sites is small enough that scoping it would have left the two `validateGitFeatures` couplings — a known instance of the same class — unproven.
+- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/`, `README.md`, `LOGBOOK.md`
+- STATUS: Resolved; TASK-260830-1tax26 handed to review as revision 6.
+
+
+### 1400 — Pinning what a gate is GIVEN does not pin what the gate DOES with it
+
