 ## 2026-09-02
 
+### 2240 — A pinned vocabulary set does not pin the JSON path that reaches it
+
+- FINDING: `testdata/closed-vocabularies.md` pins the admitted set of all 47 production vocabulary call sites and kills any widening mutant at the source. It says nothing about WHICH member each gate is reached from. A validator that read a Lease Record `reason` through the `session.quiescing` reason gate would leave every pinned row byte-identical while admitting `stop` on a lease. The pin is over the source, the defect is over the wiring.
+- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/record_conformance_test.go` walks every closed-vocabulary position of every identity fixture — 149 positions — and feeds each the union of every OTHER pinned vocabulary, 14,639 substitutions, requiring refusal outside that position's own declared set. Positions are found by VALUE, not by a member-name list, so a new enum in a new record is swept without being registered anywhere.
+- ANOMALY, and the reason the first version of this sweep was worthless: keying the declared set by member NAME let the widening mutant through. `reason` is declared twice — Lease Record `{create, graceful_takeover, force_takeover, recovery}` and `session.quiescing` `{graceful_takeover, stop, checkpoint}` — so the member-name union already contained `stop` and the sweep never substituted it. Mutant M3 SURVIVED the first run. Same shape for `kind` (session vs Transfer Manifest) and `materialization_policy` (git vs managed_tree member).
+- FIX: the declared set is now the single pinned row whose member AND admitted values contain the fixture's own value, with candidates deduplicated by admitted set so six identical `execution_profile` rows count as one vocabulary. The four genuinely ambiguous positions carry a reviewed resolution that must remain one of the candidate rows, so a widened production vocabulary invalidates the resolution instead of being absorbed by it, and a stale resolution naming no position is reported.
+- NOTE: a position whose value matches NO row for its member is an exact-literal site, not a vocabulary — `clone.failed` pins `phase=checkpoint` while `clone.target_validation_failed` carries a six-member `phase` enum. It is swept as a single-value set, which is the stronger bound, rather than skipped. Free strings that coincidentally hold a pinned value (a manifest entry whose `path` is literally `file`) fall out because no pinned row declares their member.
+- EVIDENCE: `TASK-260830-uqnwmi_mutation-evidence.log`. M3 survives the member-name-keyed sweep and dies to the value-keyed one, same fixture, same substitution set.
+- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/record_conformance_test.go`, `README.md`
+- STATUS: Resolved; TASK-260830-uqnwmi handed to review.
+
+### 2235 — Absence and grammar are different clauses, and requireExactMembers eats the first one
+
+- FINDING: mutating `validateCommonRecordEnvelope` to skip its `created_by_host_id` check when the member is ABSENT survives both the new Section 10.1 provenance sweep and the whole existing package suite. Every Section 10.1 validator calls `requireExactMembers` with its closed member list BEFORE `validateIdentityRecordEnvelope`, so an object missing the member is refused as an incomplete member set and the mutated branch is never executed at either identity production entry. Equivalent mutant, not an evidence gap.
+- FINDING, the reachable half, and it was NOT covered: mutating the same check from `requireUUIDv7` to `requireString` also survived the first draft of the sweep, because the sweep's malformed case used an empty string and `requireString` accepts it. A UUIDv4 stamped into a UUIDv7 member is well-formed JSON, well-formed UUID, and names no host — exactly the shape a forged or copied provenance stamp takes.
+- FIX: envelope mutations now include a right-typed / wrong-grammar case per member — UUIDv4 and a non-UUID string for the UUIDv7 members, an impossible calendar date for `created_at`, a non-reverse-DNS key for `extensions`. The mutant dies.
+- DECISION: the absent case is kept but its refusal REASON is pinned to `is missing required member "<member>"`, so it records which gate actually caught it instead of counting as evidence for a clause it never reached. Subsumption named and pinned rather than assumed, per the leaf's front-loaded DoD.
+- NOTE: this is the general shape behind the DoD rule "supply a COMPLETE valid member set and violate exactly one thing". A negative case that deletes a member short-circuits on the member-set gate and proves nothing about the typed gate it is named after.
+- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/record_conformance_test.go`
+- STATUS: Resolved.
+
 ### 2110 — A specification's controlled vocabulary is a mapping, not a reading
 
 - FINDING: `core_records.go` validated Session Event `predecessors` with `validateSortedUniqueDigests`, so a duplicate predecessor was **refused**. Pinned Section 5.2 (SPEC.md:1728) declares "`predecessors` as a **sorted** array of one or more record/event digests". Section 1.6 (SPEC.md:323) defines `sorted unique T[n..m]` as the COMPOUND phrase meaning "bytewise canonical ordering **and no duplicate**", and the document applies that phrase systematically — `event_heads`, `manifest_ids`, `evidence_ids`, `object_ids`, `accepted_risks`, `sanitized_remote_urls`, `agent_project_config_paths` — while using bare `sorted` for `predecessors` alone. Reading the two as synonyms invented a refusal. This was the FIFTH invented constraint on this leaf; the previous four were each fixed one at a time.
